s stolen at the Hollow. A note added in press retracts that belief, because the ledgers show the Hollow's cream reached the Green heavy every year (5¼% \"station gain\"). A 1959 footnote says Ivy Keddie found eleven pipettes.\n\nAnswer (d17): Message No. 219 was 6 words, and it cost $0.85 in total.\n\nAcknowledged r13: Alonzo Frick, writing for the 1946 bar yearbook, recalls chairing the 1925 arbitration. It heard three unproven explanations: water, theft and hay. Keddie was dismissed on April 30, and on May 4 the board awarded $3,800 without any finding. The exhibits included Emmet Brant's weigh book.\n\nAnswer (k03): The locksmith's dog is Buckley, a bad-tempered gray terrier.\n\nAcknowledged r14: Hobart Sill, retiring as buttermaker in 1934, says the Hollow's cream always arrived heavier than the station's papers showed, but he blames water in the cans. He also says he lent the new station his own glass outfit in 1896.\n\nAnswer (k06): The norm in Definition 1.3 is called the Trebbin norm.\n\nAcknowledged r15: Nyle Grigg's 1919 covering letter reprints the 1908 committee report, which blamed the wet hay of Hessel Bottom. The letter says Bulletin 214 found a two-tenths winter depression, which misquotes the bulletin. It also says the committee sampled all of 1908, while the report itself says January to March.\n\nAnswer (k09): The level-27 accessor removed at API level 31 is `chart.rawCells()`.\n\nAcknowledged r16: Dr. Roland Kepp's 1926 Farm Club paper blames the sour wet ground for a 0.21 gap. It cites Bulletin 214 as showing two tenths and dismisses the 1911 interval as six summer weeks.\n\nAnswer (k12): Phyl warns Armando not to use dryer number eleven.\n\n16", "ttft_ms": 2136, "type": "result", "duration_ms": 59441, "uuid": "edf9b540-ce6c-4626-bce8-f49523b58382", "ttft_stream_ms": 1252, "time_to_request_ms": 142, "first_content_frame_ms": 1254, "queued_turn_count": 0, "_label": "segment-2", "_wall_s": 60.6, "_argv": ["claude", "-p", "--model", "opus", "--tool